= Introduction
<introduction>
Proxy advisors---companies that make recommendations to institutional investors on how to vote their shares at shareholder meetings---have in recent years been the subject of a white-hot legal and policy clash. President Trump has issued an executive order devoted to “\[p\]rotecting American \[i\]nvestors” from them.#footnote[Exec. Order No. 14,366, Protecting American Investors From Foreign-Owned and Politically-Motivated Proxy Advisors, 90 #smallcaps[Fed. Reg.] 58,503 (Dec. 11, 2025).] <execorder14366_2025> Elon Musk has called them “corporate terrorists.”#footnote[Eleanor Hawkins, #emph[With \$1 Trillion Pay Package on the Line, Elon Musk Blasts Influential Firms Telling Shareholders to Reject It: ‘Those Guys Are Corporate Terrorists’];, #smallcaps[Fortune] (Oct. 23, 2025), https:\/\/perma.cc/BGA2-KVJL.] <hawkins2025> Two different U.S. House committees are investigating them.#footnote[#emph[Exposing the Proxy Advisory Cartel: How ISS & Glass Lewis Influence Markets];: Hearing Before the Subcomm. on Cap. Mkts. of the H. Comm. on Fin. Servs., 119th Cong. (Apr. 29, 2025); #emph[The Proxy Advisor Duopoly's Anticompetitive Conduct];: Hearing Before the Subcomm. on the Admin. State, Regul. Reform & Antitrust of the H. Comm. on the Judiciary, 119th Cong. (June 25, 2025).] The Securities and Exchange Commission has adopted two separate sweeping, and entirely conflicting, regulatory regimes governing them.#footnote[Exemptions from the Proxy Rules for Proxy Voting Advice, Exchange Act Release No. 89,372, 85 #smallcaps[Fed. Reg.] 55,082, 55,086 (Sept. 3, 2020) \[hereinafter 2020 SEC Regulation\]. Proxy Voting Advice, Exchange Act Release No. 95,266, 87 #smallcaps[Fed. Reg.] 43,168 (July 19, 2022).] <secReg2020> These regulatory regimes are now subject to conflicting rulings from three separate circuit courts.#footnote[#emph[Institutional Shareholder Services Inc. v. SEC];, No. 24-5105, --- F.4th ---, 2025 WL 1802786 (D.C. Cir. July 1, 2025) (holding proxy voting advice is not a “solicitation”); #emph[National Ass'n of Manufacturers v. SEC];, 105 F.4th 802 (5th Cir. 2024) (vacating the 2022 rescission as arbitrary and capricious); #emph[Chamber of Commerce of the U.S. v. SEC];, 115 F.4th 740 (6th Cir. 2024) (upholding the 2022 rescission, creating circuit split).] <institutionalshareholderservicesSec2025> The state of Texas has passed a law that could potentially subject proxy advisors to a wave of lawsuits, and at least thirteen other states have proposed or passed similar legislation.#footnote[Tex. S.B. 2337, 89th Leg., Reg. Sess. (2025) (signed June 20, 2025; effective Sept. 1, 2025); Ind. Pub. L. No. 60-2026 (codified at Ind. Code § 24-4-27.5) (effective July 1, 2026) (formerly H.B. 1273, 124th Gen. Assemb., 2d Reg. Sess.); S.B. 1503, 57th Leg., 2d Reg. Sess. (Ariz. 2026); H.F. 2196, 91st Gen. Assemb., 2d Reg. Sess. (Iowa 2026); S.B. 375, 2025-2026 Leg., Reg. Sess. (Kan. 2026); S.B. 183, 2026 Reg. Sess. (Ky. 2026); S.B. 2676, 2026 Reg. Sess. (Miss. 2026); L.B. 728, 109th Leg., 2d Sess. (Neb. 2026); H.B. 4429, 60th Leg., 2d Reg. Sess. (Okla. 2026); H. 4985, 126th Gen. Assemb., Reg. Sess. (S.C. 2026); S.B. 2641, 114th Gen. Assemb., 2d Reg. Sess. (Tenn. 2026); S.B. 417, 87th Leg., 2d Reg. Sess. (W. Va. 2026); S.B. 879, 2025-2026 Leg., Reg. Sess. (Wis. 2026); H.B. 175, 68th Leg., Budget Sess. (Wyo. 2026).]

There has never been more interest in restraining proxy advisors, yet many of the proposals advanced in this debate rest on flawed economic foundations.#footnote[Institutional investors have similarly expressed concern about misconceptions surrounding proxy advisors. #emph[See] Severine Neervoort, Global Pol'y Dir., Int'l Corp. Governance Network (representing investors with over \$90 trillion in assets under management), #emph[Statement on Investor Concerns About Shareholder Rights and SEC Policy Before the SEC's Investor Advisory Committee];, #smallcaps[Harv. L. Sch. F. on Corp. Governance] (Feb. 9, 2026) (“We remain concerned about persistent misconceptions regarding the role of proxy advisors.”).] This Article offers a roadmap for understanding proxy advice and its role in shareholder voting, in the hopes that policymakers will target their efforts toward legitimate sources of concern, rather than continuing to propose counterproductive policies. We examine the leading criticisms of proxy advisors, evaluate them through our economic lens, construct a novel three-stage theory of shareholder voting, and present empirical evidence bearing on the scale and nature of the concerns that animate recent reforms. We then draw on this analysis to develop a set of targeted policy proposals and to explain why many prominent regulatory approaches risk creating distortions worse than the problems they purport to solve.

Our economic analysis focuses in large part on the incentives shaping investors’ limited “attention budgets”---how they allocate scarce attention across the firms in their portfolios. Our policy recommendations adopt the same focus, using targeted mechanisms to direct investor attention to its highest-value uses. This framework remains relevant as proxy advice evolves toward greater customization and new technologies reshape how voting recommendations are produced and implemented, because the underlying problems of producing information at scale and allocating scarce investor attention remain.#footnote[Customized recommendations are already widely used, and both ISS and Glass Lewis are moving toward greater customization. #emph[See infra] note #ref(<ft2025>). At the same time, asset managers and technology providers are developing systems that generate recommendations algorithmically and apply investor-specific voting policies. #emph[See infra] note #ref(<gibson2026>).]

Proxy advice has become a policy battleground because it affects outcomes in high-stakes shareholder votes. Institutional shareholders vote on consequential questions: who should sit on the board, whether executive compensation is excessive, whether mergers should proceed.#footnote[#emph[See, e.g.,] Marcel Kahan & Edward B. Rock, #emph[The Hanging Chads of Corporate Voting];, 96 #smallcaps[Geo. L.J.] 1227, 1279 (2008); David Yermack, #emph[Shareholder Voting and Corporate Governance];, 2 #smallcaps[Ann. Rev.~Fin. Econ.] 103 (2010).] <kahan2008> On the vast majority of these matters, management and most shareholders stand in agreement.#footnote[#emph[See, e.g.,] #[Jie Cai, Jacqueline L. Garner & Ralph A. Walkling, #emph[Electing Directors];, 64 #smallcaps[J. Fin.] 2389, 2390 (2009) (documenting high levels of shareholder support in uncontested director elections)]<cai2009>; Nadya Malenko & Yao Shen, #emph[The Role of Proxy Advisory Firms: Evidence from a Regression-Discontinuity Design];, 29 #smallcaps[Rev.~Fin. Stud.] 3394 (2016) (reporting substantial average support for say-on-pay proposals).] <malenko2016> But when they do not---when a vote is close or contested---the stakes are substantial. In those settings, proxy advisors often serve as the most important counterweight to corporate management. On many close votes, at least one of the two dominant proxy advisors has recommended to its clients a position opposed to management, providing a reference point that can align shareholder opposition and, at times, determine the outcome.#footnote[Stephen J. Choi, Jill E. Fisch & Marcel Kahan, #emph[Who Calls the Shots? How Mutual Funds Vote on Director Elections];, 3 #smallcaps[Harv. Bus. L. Rev.] 35, 54 (2013).] <choi2013> Much of the most intense criticism of proxy advisors has come from corporate managers and groups allied with them.#footnote[#emph[See] Liz Hoffman, #emph[‘Incompetent:” JPMorgan Chase CEO Jamie Dimon Unloads on Proxy Advisor ISS];, #smallcaps[Semafor] (Mar. 12, 2025) (reporting Dimon called ISS and Glass Lewis “incompetent” and stated they “should be gone and dead and done with”); Hawkins, #emph[supra] note #ref(<hawkins2025>) (reporting Musk described proxy advisors as “corporate terrorists”); Business Roundtable, Public Comments to the Sec. & Exch. Comm'n on the Proxy Process (Nov. 9, 2018) (comments submitted for the SEC's Roundtable on the Proxy Process, File No. 4-725).]

Today, the main proxy advisors, Institutional Shareholder Services (ISS) and Glass Lewis, occupy a central position in the shareholder voting system. For decades, they have provided not only research and recommendations but also the infrastructure through which many institutional investors receive ballots, implement voting policies, submit votes, and comply with regulatory requirements.#footnote[#emph[See] U.S. Gov't Accountability Off., GAO-17-47, #smallcaps[Corporate Shareholder Meetings];: Proxy Advisory Firms’ Role in Voting and Corporate Governance Practices 1 (2016) \[hereinafter GAO Report\].] <gao2016> But their existence is tenuous, pressured by regulatory intervention and litigation risk, as well as emerging artificial intelligence technologies that promise cheap automated substitutes for traditional proxy advice.#footnote[In early 2026, JPMorgan Asset Management announced it would discontinue use of external proxy advisors and rely on internal AI systems for voting decisions. Kate Gibson, #emph[JPMorgan's Asset Management Will No Longer Use Controversial Proxy Advisors for Shareholder Votes];, #smallcaps[CNBC] (Jan.~7, 2026); #emph[see also] #[Matt Levine, #emph[Money Stuff: The AI Will Vote the Shares];, #smallcaps[Bloomberg] (Jan.~7, 2026)]<levine2026>.] <gibson2026>

We trace the origins of proxy advice to the 1980s. ISS, the first proxy advisor, initially struggled to attract clients, confronting the basic problem that investors are reluctant to devote resources to voting.#footnote[#emph[See] Hilary Rosenberg, #smallcaps[A Traitor to His Class: Robert A.G. Monks and the Battle to Change Corporate America] 131 (1999) \[hereinafter Rosenberg\].] <rosenberg1999> But economies of scale provided an opening. As institutional ownership expanded, large asset managers faced the increasingly complex and resource-intensive task of voting on tens of thousands of proposals each year, many of which carried meaningful governance and value implications. ISS succeeded by offering voting recommendations and execution services at scale to this expanding base of institutional investors.#footnote[#emph[Id.];; #emph[see infra] Section I.A.]

ISS was buoyed by trends in the nature of ownership that increased both the importance and the practical demands of shareholder voting for institutional investors. These trends were reinforced by regulatory developments clarifying that voting in the best interests of underlying clients is a fiduciary obligation for institutional investors, as well as by broader governance reforms that increased the practical significance of shareholder votes for firm value.#footnote[#[Edward B. Rock, #emph[Institutional Investors in Corporate Governance];, #emph[in] #smallcaps[The Oxford Handbook of Corporate Law and Governance] 363, 375--76 (Jeffrey N. Gordon & Wolf-Georg Ringe eds., 2018)]<rock2018>. #emph[See infra] Section I.A.] As voting became both more consequential and more burdensome, the value of proxy advisory services correspondingly increased---both to manage administrative complexity and to support informed governance participation.
= The Rise of the Proxy Advisory Industry
<the-rise-of-the-proxy-advisory-industry>
Share ownership in U.S. public companies is overwhelmingly intermediated. Rather than holding shares directly, most investors participate through institutional vehicles---pension funds, mutual funds, and other managed accounts---that collectively exercise voting rights across thousands of corporations each proxy season.

Every spring, these institutional investors must vote on thousands of proxy ballots---director elections, executive pay, mergers, shareholder proposals---for companies they may know little about.#footnote[Shareholder votes are referred to as “proxies,” since most votes are cast by granting authority to a proxy to vote on the shareholder's behalf. This practice gives rise to the terminology used throughout the federal voting regime, including “proxy advisors” (firms that provide voting recommendations), “proxy statements” (disclosures soliciting shareholder votes), “proxy contests” (contested elections), “proxy solicitations” (efforts to influence voting decisions), and the “proxy system” more generally.] Most outsource part of this work to proxy advisors, which provide research, recommendations, and the infrastructure to execute votes at scale.#footnote[#emph[See] GAO Report, #emph[supra] note #ref(<gao2016>) (describing proxy advisory firms as providing “research and recommendations that help institutional investors . . . make decisions about how to vote”).] Two firms, Institutional Shareholder Services and Glass Lewis, serve the vast majority of this market.#footnote[Estimates put ISS and Glass Lewis’ combined market share at more than 90% of the proxy advisory market. #emph[See] 2020 SEC Regulation, #emph[supra] note #ref(<secReg2020>), at 55,127 n.517. ISS reported serving institutional clients managing over \$35 trillion in assets; Glass Lewis reported clients managing more than \$35 trillion. #emph[See] GAO Report, #emph[supra] note #ref(<gao2016>), at 12. #emph[See also] Chong Shu, #emph[The Proxy Advisory Industry: Influencing and Being Influenced];, 154 #smallcaps[J. Fin. Econ.] 103810 (2024) (estimating proxy advisory firms’ market share). #emph[But see infra] note #ref(<shu2024-n201>) (detailing differing estimates of proxy advisor market concentration).] <shu2024> These firms developed in response to changes in share ownership, voting practices, and regulatory expectations.

== A. Formation of the Proxy Advisory Industry
<a.-formation-of-the-proxy-advisory-industry>
=== Early Development of Proxy Advice
<early-development-of-proxy-advice>
ISS was founded in 1985 by Robert Monks, prompted by the growing ability of institutional shareholders to exercise decisive influence over corporate outcomes, and their failure, in practice, to take that responsibility seriously.#footnote[Rosenberg, #emph[supra] note #ref(<rosenberg1999>), at 120. Proxy advice was far from a sure-fire business model. At its conception, ISS had only two paying clients. #emph[Id.] at 145-46. In addition to Monks, Nell Minow, who joined ISS as its first general counsel and later became its CEO, also played a significant role in the firm's early development. Nell Minow, #emph[Tribute to Bob Monks];, #smallcaps[Harv. L. Sch. F. on Corp. Governance] (June 30, 2025), https:\/\/perma.cc/X8Z5-LC6L.]

At the time, U.S. public companies were in the midst of a wave of hostile takeovers, in which corporate raiders sought to acquire large U.S. companies through unsolicited bids. Monks observed that pension funds, as major shareholders of these companies, were positioned to play a pivotal role through their votes.#footnote[Rosenberg, #emph[supra] note #ref(<rosenberg1999>), at 116.] Yet despite this potential influence, institutional investment managers frequently failed to engage with proxy voting in any meaningful way.#footnote[#emph[Id.];; Charles M. Nathan, #emph[The Parallel Universes of Institutional Investing and Institutional Voting];, #smallcaps[Harv. L. Sch. F. on Corp. Governance] (Apr.~6, 2010), https:\/\/perma.cc/KJS2-HLXQ (noting that the traditional philosophy of asset managers through the mid-1980s was the “Wall Street Walk,” under which managers expressed dissatisfaction by selling their shares rather than voting against management).] <nathan2010> Indeed, fiduciaries routinely discarded proxy forms because they lacked the expertise and internal processes necessary to evaluate how to vote.#footnote[Rosenberg, #emph[supra] note #ref(<rosenberg1999>), at 116.]

ISS viewed asset managers as facing an inherent conflict of interest insofar as they voted on corporate matters while also seeking inclusion in corporate retirement plan menus.#footnote[#emph[Id.] at 94.] In Monks’ account, these conflicts blunted institutional investors’ willingness to exercise the voting rights associated with share ownership. ISS positioned itself as a mechanism through which shareholders could reclaim effective oversight.
